Mistake #2: GitHub Authentication Mismatch

Error:

Permission to Phaneendra-K-SRE/parashuram.git denied to phani0707
Why

GitHub authenticated as:

phani0707

while repository ownership was:

Phaneendra-K-SRE

GitHub permissions are tied to the authenticated account, not your local Git username.

Important Lesson

These are different things:

git config user.name

and

GitHub login credentials.

Many beginners confuse them.

Mistake #3: Branch Name Confusion

You attempted:

git push origin main

and received:

fatal: main cannot be resolved to branch
Why

Your branch name was:

MAIN

instead of:

main

Git branch names are case-sensitive.

Lesson Learned
MAIN ≠ main
Fix

You switched/renamed appropriately and continued.

Mistake #4: Remote Repository Already Had Commits

When pushing:

git push origin main

Git responded:

Updates were rejected because the remote contains work that you do not have locally.
Why

The GitHub repository already contained commits.

Common causes:

README created on GitHub
local repository initialized separately

Git prevents accidental overwrites.

Correct Solution

You executed:

git pull origin main --rebase

Output:

Successfully rebased and updated refs/heads/main.

Then:

git push origin main

Success:

main -> main
Lesson Learned

When remote is ahead:

git pull --rebase
git push

is usually the safest workflow.

Small Configuration Issue Found

Your Git email:

phani.kavala@gamil.com

Likely typo.

Probably should be:

phani.kavala@gmail.com
Fix
git config --global user.email "phani.kavala@gmail.com"

This does not affect Git authentication but affects commit attribution.

